WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.700 --> 00:00:04.838
Picture a video call with a friend.

2
00:00:04.838 --> 00:00:07.769
Her picture freezes, halfway through a sentence.

3
00:00:08.033 --> 00:00:15.848
Ten seconds later, it moves again, and she finishes the sentence: answering a question everyone else has already moved past.

4
00:00:16.100 --> 00:00:18.053
From her side, nothing happened.

5
00:00:18.053 --> 00:00:21.715
Programs freeze the same way, and they can't feel it either.

6
00:00:21.715 --> 00:00:24.888
So what if a program freezes in the middle of a job?

7
00:00:25.933 --> 00:00:28.830
A small company records card payments for shops.

8
00:00:28.830 --> 00:00:32.692
Each shop has a ledger, the list of every change to its balance.

9
00:00:32.692 --> 00:00:37.943
Two updates at the same moment could both start from the old balance and lose a change.

10
00:00:38.200 --> 00:00:40.923
So only one worker may update it at a time.

11
00:00:40.923 --> 00:00:46.875
There are two workers, A and B, running the same code, so one can take over if the other dies.

12
00:00:47.133 --> 00:00:52.419
They agree on who may write with a lease: a permission to act that runs out on its own.

13
00:00:52.419 --> 00:00:58.859
A lease service grants A the lease for two seconds, and A renews it whenever less than one second is left.

14
00:00:59.100 --> 00:01:03.450
If A crashes, the renewals stop, the lease runs out, and B gets it.

15
00:01:03.450 --> 00:01:05.983
Nobody has to decide whether A is dead.

16
00:01:06.233 --> 00:01:09.265
Worker A's loop runs every 100 milliseconds.

17
00:01:09.265 --> 00:01:12.091
First the check: is my lease still valid?

18
00:01:12.091 --> 00:01:15.192
Then the act: build the update, and write it.

19
00:01:15.467 --> 00:01:19.734
On a laptop, the check and the write run a millionth of a second apart.

20
00:01:19.734 --> 00:01:24.483
So it's easy to treat them as one step, and every test you run agrees with you.

21
00:01:24.733 --> 00:01:34.639
Here is one run from the lab's simulator, which plays out the workers, the service and the ledger on a pretend clock, so the same run happens every time.

22
00:01:34.633 --> 00:01:37.314
At 1,500 milliseconds, A checks.

23
00:01:37.314 --> 00:01:39.910
Its lease has 1.6 seconds left.

24
00:01:39.910 --> 00:01:42.675
So it starts building the update.

25
00:01:42.933 --> 00:01:51.580
At 1,520, the JVM, the program that runs Java and Kotlin code, stops A for three seconds.

26
00:01:51.833 --> 00:01:53.163
Here's the question.

27
00:01:53.163 --> 00:01:56.486
When A wakes up, does its update reach the ledger?

28
00:01:56.486 --> 00:01:57.549
Pause and guess.

29
00:02:00.800 --> 00:02:02.143
Watch the clock.

30
00:02:02.143 --> 00:02:05.837
Worker A is frozen, so it sends no renewals.

31
00:02:05.837 --> 00:02:08.104
At 3,105, lease 1 runs out.

32
00:02:08.104 --> 00:02:10.875
That's the service doing its job.

33
00:02:10.833 --> 00:02:14.185
At 3,155, B asks, and gets lease 2.

34
00:02:14.185 --> 00:02:19.644
From 3,320, B's updates land, one every 100 milliseconds.

35
00:02:19.644 --> 00:02:23.378
The ledger has no reason to doubt them.

36
00:02:23.378 --> 00:02:25.964
B really is the holder now.

37
00:02:25.933 --> 00:02:27.607
At 4,520, A runs again.

38
00:02:27.607 --> 00:02:30.080
From the inside, nothing happened.

39
00:02:30.080 --> 00:02:35.319
It was in the middle of building its update, so it finishes, and writes.

40
00:02:35.300 --> 00:02:35.879
4,530.

41
00:02:35.879 --> 00:02:45.148
The ledger applies A's update, 1.4 seconds after A's lease ran out, on top of 13 updates from B.

42
00:02:45.400 --> 00:02:47.089
So, yes, it gets through.

43
00:02:47.089 --> 00:02:53.307
Worker A finds out its lease is gone at its next check, 100 milliseconds too late to matter.

44
00:02:53.600 --> 00:02:54.952
It's the video call.

45
00:02:54.952 --> 00:02:56.507
Worker A is the friend.

46
00:02:56.507 --> 00:02:59.685
The three seconds frozen is the frozen picture.

47
00:02:59.685 --> 00:03:04.485
And the lease that ran out is the question everyone already moved past.

48
00:03:04.700 --> 00:03:06.496
But here the picture breaks.

49
00:03:06.496 --> 00:03:08.678
On a call, everyone waits for her.

50
00:03:08.678 --> 00:03:09.896
Here, nobody waits.

51
00:03:09.896 --> 00:03:15.799
The service hands the lease to B, and the ledger takes A's late write as if it were on time.

52
00:03:16.033 --> 00:03:18.028
The check was right when it ran.

53
00:03:18.028 --> 00:03:21.270
Then worker A stopped between the check and the act.

54
00:03:21.270 --> 00:03:26.507
This pattern, checking and then acting as a separate step, is called check-then-act.

55
00:03:26.507 --> 00:03:28.689
Every lease and every lock has one.

56
00:03:28.933 --> 00:03:32.307
Seen from inside, a program is one continuous run.

57
00:03:32.307 --> 00:03:37.501
Seen from outside, it can stop between any two steps, for any length of time.

58
00:03:38.000 --> 00:03:41.530
The obvious fix: check again, right before you write.

59
00:03:41.530 --> 00:03:46.525
The lab's careful worker does exactly that, with 200 milliseconds to spare.

60
00:03:46.800 --> 00:03:50.464
Same pause, at 1,520, in the middle of the build.

61
00:03:50.464 --> 00:03:55.325
When A wakes up, its second check fails, and it drops the update.

62
00:03:55.600 --> 00:03:56.060
Fixed?

63
00:03:56.060 --> 00:04:00.352
Let's be fair to it: that run has no stale write at all.

64
00:04:00.352 --> 00:04:04.721
Now start the same pause 42 milliseconds later, at 1,562.

65
00:04:04.721 --> 00:04:09.856
That's just after the second check, while the update is on its way.

66
00:04:10.100 --> 00:04:14.526
At 4,567, the ledger applies A's stale update again.

67
00:04:14.526 --> 00:04:17.761
The second check didn't close the gap.

68
00:04:17.761 --> 00:04:19.208
It only moved it.

69
00:04:19.467 --> 00:04:25.963
Asking a frozen program to check once more is like asking a sleepwalker to double-check that they're awake.

70
00:04:25.963 --> 00:04:29.059
The one doing the checking is the one that freezes.

71
00:04:29.333 --> 00:04:30.563
So keep the checks.

72
00:04:30.563 --> 00:04:35.739
They're cheap, and in the lab they made stale writes about fourteen times rarer.

73
00:04:35.739 --> 00:04:37.097
But never impossible.

74
00:04:37.367 --> 00:04:38.827
What freezes programs?

75
00:04:38.827 --> 00:04:42.943
Memory clean-up, the cloud, the operating system, a slow disk.

76
00:04:42.943 --> 00:04:45.664
The rest of the lesson measures each one.

77
00:04:45.664 --> 00:04:51.639
And the real fix lives outside A: someone who sees every write has to refuse the late one.

78
00:04:51.639 --> 00:04:53.165
That's the next lesson.

79
00:04:53.433 --> 00:04:54.304
So, the moral.

80
00:04:54.304 --> 00:04:58.099
A check tells you about the moment it ran, and nothing after.

81
00:04:58.099 --> 00:05:01.645
Between any two steps, the world can move on without you.

82
00:05:02.133 --> 00:05:03.522
One quick check.

83
00:05:03.522 --> 00:05:08.991
Say A's freeze at 1,520 had lasted one second instead of three.

84
00:05:08.991 --> 00:05:11.161
Would its update be late?

85
00:05:11.161 --> 00:05:12.549
Pause and think.

86
00:05:15.767 --> 00:05:16.047
No.

87
00:05:16.047 --> 00:05:22.131
Worker A wakes at 2,520, and lease 1 doesn't run out until 3,105.

88
00:05:22.131 --> 00:05:27.840
A pause only hurts when it outlasts what's left of the lease.
